msgid "Introduction"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"As LLMs become integral to enterprise applications, securing the prompts "
"they receive is critical."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"https://docs.quarkiverse.io/quarkus-langchain4j/dev/index.html[Quarkus "
"LangChain4j] is a Quarkus extension that allows\n"
"users to integrate AI into applications, and the\n"
"https://quarkus.io/quarkus-workshop-langchain4j/[Quarkus LangChain4j "
"Workshop] is a good way to learn about and practice\n"
"the fundamentals."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Regarding security, https://quarkus.io/quarkus-workshop-langchain4j/section-"
"1/step-09[Step 9] describes _input\n"
"guardrails_, showcasing an example of how a separate LLM can be used to "
"detect _prompt injection_ attacks.\n"
"However, this approach has costs, both in terms of tokens and performance."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Deterministic pattern matching can catch obvious attacks faster, and "
"delegating to the LLM becomes the last option,\n"
"thus reducing the aforementioned effects."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"At the same time, https://www.openpolicyagent.org/[Open Policy Agent] can be "
"used to define declarative policies\n"
"that can be externalized."
msgstr ""

msgid "This post shows how to combine both approaches in Quarkus LangChain4j."
msgstr ""

msgid "Open Policy Agent (OPA)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Pattern matching against a regex is straightforward, but the usual "
"limitations apply:"
msgstr ""

msgid "rules are subject to change"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"rules are usually defined by someone who is not strictly an application "
"developer"
msgstr ""

msgid "rules should be defined independently of the application lifecycle."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This is where https://www.openpolicyagent.org/[Open Policy Agent] comes to "
"help.\n"
"It provides a framework that streamlines policy management across "
"application deployment technologies, resources and\n"
"roles."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In our specific use case, deterministic guardrails can be defined as OPA "
"policies, decoupling them from the application\n"
"logic, e.g.:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The above https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs/latest/policy-language/[Rego] "
"snippet defines an OPA policy that parses\n"
"the prompt to find either exact or regex matches of malicious content."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Deterministic input guardrails could use such policy to detect a prompt "
"injection attack, and the policy can be\n"
"updated by the security team, independently of the application business "
"logic and lifecycle."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Now, how to quickly integrate OPA policy evaluation into our example Java "
"application?"
msgstr ""

